#{[17592186380048 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$"] [17592186380055 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$"] [17592186380062 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186380242 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186381174 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x(y+h)).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x(y+h))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186381176 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x(y+h))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381177 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x(y-h))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381178 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7(x+h)(y+h))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381179 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381180 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xyh)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381188 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={x}^{y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)={x}^{y+h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186381190 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y-h}+{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381191 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{yh}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381192 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381193 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381194 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186381881 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{5}+8}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186386082 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 5}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 5}f(x)\\ne f(5)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the second condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=5$ only."] [17592186386719 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3n}]"] [17592186386720 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186386721 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186386722 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186386723 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186386725 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186386726 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186386727 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2n}]"] [17592186386728 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186386729 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186386732 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186386733 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186386734 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{3n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186386735 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186386736 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3n}]"] [17592186390841 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to -2}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨(s+3)^{1/(s+2)},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(s+2)}{(s+2)},\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+2s}{{s}^{2}+3s+2}⟩.\n$$"] [17592186392103 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2"] [17592186392104 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186392105 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=-1"] [17592186392106 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1"] [17592186392107 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0"] [17592186392678 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186392679 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186392680 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186392681 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186392682 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[2{(3+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186392684 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186392685 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186392686 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186392687 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[-{(2+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186392688 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186393071 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-3)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)\\ne f(-3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nA similar argument applies for $x=3,$ which is also a removable discontinuity. \n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=3$ only."] [17592186393252 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{4ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4e}{n}]"] [17592186393253 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(-2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186393254 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}]"] [17592186393255 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186393256 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}]"] [17592186393259 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{1}{16}{\\left(\\frac{3l}{n}\\right)}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393260 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4l}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393261 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(4+\\frac{l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186393262 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[16{\\left(\\frac{7l}{n}\\right)}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{n}]"] [17592186393263 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186393266 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{\\left(\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186393267 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186393268 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186393269 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-{(-1+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186393270 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2(5+\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186393273 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393274 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393275 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393276 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186393277 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186396620 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186396620 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$"] [17592186396634 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\""] [17592186396654 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""] [17592186396680 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186396680 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"] [17592186396792 :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,3x=O(3x)$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{L2}:\\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2x}\\right)=1\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}."] [17592186396818 :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}."] [17592186397587 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)"] [17592186398834 :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$"] [17592186399440 :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$"] [17592186402402 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$"] [17592186402497 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186402498 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186402499 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186402500 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186402501 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186402662 :question/worked-solution "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” is false, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$” is true and “It's continuous at $x=0.$” is false."] [17592186402675 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$"] [17592186402881 :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nOn the other hand, the function has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\ne f(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “There are removable discontinuities at both $x=-1$ and $x=1.$” are false."] [17592186402888 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)\\ne f(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” and “It has removable discontinuities at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$” are true while “It is continuous at $x=3.$” is false."] [17592186402936 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false."] [17592186402943 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-2.$” are false."] [17592186403020 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)"] [17592186403022 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186403023 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)"] [17592186403265 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b0074bb582011695cec9807beab1623c6aab894e0c6abeb1bf3068be6ed3543.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}\\frac{\\sqrt{25-f(x)}}{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186407846 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$"] [17592186407847 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186407848 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$"]}
